  "query_preview": "..Today through Thursday...As the ridge to our east \nbegins to retrograde, warming aloft will act to limit much of the \nstorm potential over northern Arizona through the next couple of \ndays. While moisture does remain, PWATS around 0.75 to 1.00 \ninches, the threat is not totally removed. Daily chances for \nisolated to scattered storms look to continue, mainly over the \nMogollon Rim and White Mountains today, increasing in coverage \nslightly by Wednesday. Any activity that does develop will be \nfairly minor, and isolated in nature, as the cap aloft will \ninhibit any substantial convective development. Coverage looks to \nfurther increase on Thursday, especially along and south of the \nMogollon Rim. \n\nFriday through Monday...As a trough mover through the Pacific \nNorthwest, moisture looks to be pulled northward through the day \nFriday. PWATs look to increase to around an inch across the Four \nCorners, to near 1.40-1.50 inches across central Arizona. Guidance \nas of now has the greatest potential for storms on Friday, initially \nnorth of the Mogollon Rim during the afternoon. Storms look to then \nmove off the higher terrain, southwestward into central Arizona \nthrough the evening and overnight hours. This overnight activity \nlooks to be aided by a weak shortwave passing to the north, \npotentially adding some upper-level dynamics to support the \nnocturnal convection. This in turn will likely lead to a heightened \nflash flood risk Friday afternoon into Saturday morning.\n\nAs the trough continues to move eastward, the ridge becomes \nsuppressed further south. Confidence beyond this period remains low, \nas the operational guidance continues to flip flop in the exact \nsetup. However, more of the EPS, CMCE, and GEFS members do seem to \nbe in agreement with a decrease in moisture late in the weekend into \nearly next week. Thus, a downward tick in thunderstorm potential \nseems to be the most likely scenario at this time, however thing \ncould certainly change. Otherwise, breezier and cooler conditions \nlook to set in across northern Arizona over the weekend as the ridge \nloosens it's grip.",
